  "why_the_first_control_was_replaced": "the first version permuted the response ONCE and asserted all four terms had p > 0.01. Under the null each term has a 1% chance of falling below 0.01, so that control fails spuriously about 4% of the time -- it is flaky, and a flaky control is worse than none because it trains the reader to ignore failures. It did fail, on seed at p = 0.0037. Replacing a single draw with a 300-draw calibration (null mean F must be within 15% of the theoretical 1.0, and the false-positive rate at alpha = 0.05 must sit in 0.00-0.12) measures the same property stably.",
